(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-dermatology-pathophys-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatology Pathophysiology Quiz 2 — Clinical Pathophysiology I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which receptors carry the sensations of touch and pressure?","choices":["Golgi tendon organs and muscle spindles","Langerhans cells and melanocytes","Meissner's corpuscles, Pacinian corpuscles and Merkel cells","Ruffini endings, Krause bulbs and free nerve endings only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Meissner's serve fine discriminative touch and low-frequency vibration, Pacinian respond to deep pressure and vibration, and Merkel cells serve light touch. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are the two phases of allergic contact dermatitis?","choices":["Irritation, then hyperkeratosis of the stratum corneum","Radial growth, then vertical growth","Sensitization, as Langerhans cells present haptens to T cells to form memory; then elicitation, as re-exposure drives T cell migration and inflammation","Sensitization by mast cell degranulation, followed by elicitation through the release of histamine into the surrounding dermal tissue on every subsequent exposure to that same allergen thereafter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Langerhans cells train T cells against a hapten, and re-exposure triggers T cell migration and inflammation, a delayed reaction at 48 to 72 hours. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which description of the pathology of nail clubbing is accurate?","choices":["Incomplete keratinization at the nail bed","Increased capillary density, with more vascular endothelial growth factor release","Overgrowth of the connective tissue within the nail bed itself over a period of time","A halt of keratin production at the matrix"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The main pathologic finding in clubbing is increased capillary density, with increased release of vascular endothelial growth factor. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which mechanism drives psoriasis?","choices":["Melanocytes proliferate at the dermal-epidermal junction","Active T cells in the epidermis drive keratinocyte proliferation and excess cytokines, such as tumor necrosis factor alpha","Mast cells release histamine which drives keratinocyte proliferation","Langerhans cells present haptens to T cells, producing a delayed hypersensitivity reaction 48 to 72 hours after each exposure to the allergen in question every single time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Active T cells infiltrate the epidermis, stimulating keratinocyte proliferation and excess cytokines (tumor necrosis factor alpha, interferon gamma and interleukin-12), so skin cells multiply up to ten times faster. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which description of seborrheic dermatitis is accurate?","choices":["Microbiome dysbiosis, altered immunity and barrier dysfunction, in the sebaceous-rich scalp, face and upper trunk","A single fungal pathogen acting alone, affecting mainly the palms of the hands and the soles of the feet rather than the sebaceous areas of the skin","Autoimmune T cell activation, affecting the elbows and knees","Ultraviolet damage, affecting sun-exposed skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cause is incompletely understood, but dysbiosis, altered immunity and barrier dysfunction contribute, in sebaceous-rich scalp, face and upper trunk. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which description of lichenification is accurate?","choices":["Loss of melanocytes across a circumscribed area","Separation of the epidermis from the dermis with accumulation of fluid between the two layers, forming a large blister that lifts the surface away","Slowing of keratinocyte division with elastin degradation","Long-term scratching or rubbing in an itch-scratch cycle, causing hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis in thick plaques without scaling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The itch-scratch cycle drives hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis into firm, thick plaques without scaling, as in lichen simplex chronicus. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which factors cause bullae?","choices":["Capillary rupture and thrombocytopenia","Androgen stimulation of sebaceous glands","Mechanical factors, infections, toxins, chemicals and autoimmunity","Increased melanin production and cumulative ultraviolet light exposure alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with bullous impetigo as the bacterial example and bullous pemphigoid as the autoimmune one. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A patient has thick, leathery plaques on the neck and forearms with no scaling, after months of scratching. Which secondary lesion is this, mechanistically?","choices":["Ulcer, arising from focal loss of both the epidermis and the dermis beneath it","Atrophy, from slowed keratinocyte division","Lichenification, from itch-scratch-driven hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis","Scale, from desquamating stratum corneum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The absence of scaling is what separates it from psoriasis, whose plaques do scale. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What are the growth phases of melanoma, in order?","choices":["Vertical growth down into the dermis first, then radial spread within the epidermis afterwards once the tumor has thickened sufficiently to invade","Inflammatory, proliferative, then remodeling","Sensitization then elicitation","Radial growth in the epidermis, vertical growth into the dermis, thickening, then lymphatic and vascular invasion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Melanoma cells first multiply within the epidermis, then penetrate the dermis, grow deeper and thicker, and finally invade lymphatics and blood vessels. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which description of the proliferative phase of wound healing is accurate?","choices":["A fibrin hemostatic plug, over days 1 to 3","Type I collagen laid down in parallel bundles, over days 21 to one year of remodeling","Keratohyalin granules within the granular layer","Granulation tissue of macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelial cells, days 4 to 21"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Over days 4 to 21, granulation tissue forms, comprised of macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelial cells, before remodeling begins. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the suspected mechanism of koilonychia?","choices":["Impaired keratin synthesis with deficiency anemia, leaving a thin, concave nail plate","A halt of keratin production producing horizontal transverse grooves across the surface of the nail plate itself","Overgrowth of connective tissue producing an opaque nail","Incomplete keratinization producing white spots"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Koilonychia is thought to reflect impaired keratin synthesis with deficiency anemia, leaving a weakened, thin, concave nail; the exact pathophysiology is unknown. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which layer forms the semi-impermeable barrier of the skin?","choices":["The papillary dermis, a thin superficial connective tissue layer","The stratum corneum, 20 to 30 layers of keratin and dead keratinocytes cemented together","The stratum basale, a single layer of rapidly dividing columnar keratinocytes at the base","The stratum granulosum, 3 to 5 layers of diamond-shaped cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The stratum corneum, the outermost layer, packs 20 to 30 layers of keratin and dead keratinocytes into a semi-impermeable barrier. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which protein deficiency contributes to atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Insufficient collagen, reflecting fibroblast failure","Insufficient elastin, reflecting loss of recoil","Insufficient keratohyalin, reflecting loss of the granular layer","Insufficient filaggrin, reflecting a compromised epidermal barrier"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Insufficient filaggrin, due to a compromised epidermal barrier, combines with an overactive immune system and genetic, environmental and psychogenic factors. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of urticaria?","choices":["T cell-mediated delayed hypersensitivity 48 to 72 hours after exposure","Dysplastic keratinocyte proliferation from ultraviolet damage","Capillary rupture with extravasation of blood","Mast cell degranulation releasing histamine, producing wheals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In urticaria, mast cells release histamine, which causes vasodilation and plasma leakage into the dermis, forming wheals. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What gives the spinous layer its alternative name of prickle cell layer?","choices":["Keratohyalin granules that give the cells a rough and irregular outline when viewed on microscopy","Melanin granules transferred from melanocytes","Dead flattened keratinocytes packed into scales","Keratinocytes connected by intercellular connections called desmosomes, which form a spiny appearance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The 8 to 10 keratinocyte layers of the stratum spinosum are joined by desmosomes, which give the spiny look behind the name prickle cell layer. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which description of an epidermal inclusion cyst is accurate?","choices":["A sebaceous gland, and it contains only squalene","A blood vessel, and it contains clotted blood","A hair follicle, and it contains keratin","A sweat gland, and it contains sodium and chloride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An epidermal inclusion cyst arises from a hair follicle and contains keratin, enclosed in a capsule within the dermis or subcutaneous layer. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which description of how dermatophytes infect the skin is accurate?","choices":["They proliferate within the dermis, digesting collagen","Spores secrete keratinases and proteases that digest keratin, typically in the stratum corneum","They invade epidermal basal cells through microabrasions and drive proliferation of the epidermis above them over time","They release histamine from mast cells producing wheals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dermatophyte spores secrete keratinases and proteases that digest keratin in skin, hair and nails, and infection typically occurs in the stratum corneum. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"How do eccrine and apocrine sweat glands differ in secretion and function?","choices":["Eccrine secrete keratin and apocrine secrete melanin","Eccrine secrete water and electrolytes for evaporative cooling; apocrine, mainly axillary and anogenital, secrete protein and lipids as scent glands","Eccrine glands sit in the axilla and anogenital areas and secrete protein and fatty lipids; apocrine glands open onto the surface of the skin and secrete water and electrolytes for cooling the body","Both secrete sebum, differing only in the body regions they occupy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Eccrine glands open onto the skin surface and cool the body by evaporation; body odor comes from bacterial degradation of apocrine sweat, not from the sweat itself. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which description of the origin of melanoma is accurate?","choices":["Within the stratum corneum, transformed by keratinase digestion","In the reticular layer of the dermis, transformed by dysregulation of the fibroblasts that reside within it over a long period of time","In the basal layer, transformed by Hedgehog pathway activation","At the dermal-epidermal junction, with ultraviolet light and oxidative stress damaging melanocyte DNA and leading to uninhibited growth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Melanoma arises from melanocytes, found at the dermal-epidermal junction, when ultraviolet light and oxidative stress damage their DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid). — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"How is melanoma risk inherited?","choices":["As an autosomal dominant trait with variable penetrance","As an autosomal recessive trait with complete penetrance","As an X-linked trait","It is entirely environmental with no inherited component"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, though ultraviolet light and oxidative stress remain the primary driving factor. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A patient reacts to a nickel earring 48 to 72 hours after wearing it. Which mechanism does the delay reflect?","choices":["Viral proliferation within basal keratinocytes","Delayed hypersensitivity: T cells migrate after re-exposure to a hapten once presented by Langerhans cells","An immediate immunoglobulin E-mediated mast cell degranulation occurring upon the very first contact with the metal","Direct chemical injury requiring no prior exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The delay is the time the cellular response takes, which is what distinguishes it from an immediate mast cell reaction. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which description of Beau's lines is accurate?","choices":["Increased capillary density, associated with lung disease","Impaired keratin synthesis, associated with deficiency anemia","A halt of keratin production causing transverse grooves, with illness, stress, nail injury or malnourishment","Overgrowth of connective tissue within the nail bed, associated with chronic liver disease and with diabetes mellitus in particular"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beau's lines are horizontal or transverse grooves caused by a halt of keratin production, associated with illness, stress, nail injury and malnourishment. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which skin cancer is the most common malignancy in humans?","choices":["Actinic keratosis, which is by far the most common malignancy occurring in human beings","Basal cell carcinoma, slow growing and rarely metastasizing, typically on the head and neck","Squamous cell carcinoma, rapidly metastasizing","Melanoma, slow growing and rarely metastasizing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Basal cell carcinoma is the most common skin cancer and most common human malignancy; it grows slowly and rarely metastasizes. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which description of Terry's nails is accurate?","choices":["Overgrowth of connective tissue in the nail bed, associated with aging, liver disease, congestive heart failure or diabetes","Increased capillary density together with vascular endothelial growth factor release into the tissue locally over time","Impaired keratin synthesis with deficiency anemia","Temporary incomplete keratinization at the nail bed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Terry's nails come from overgrowth of connective tissue in the nail bed, leaving the nail opaque except for a dark band at the tip. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Why does an ulcer scar while an erosion generally does not?","choices":["An ulcer destroys dermal collagen, which healing must replace; an erosion spares the dermis","An ulcer is infected and an erosion is sterile","An ulcer is larger, and size alone determines scarring","An ulcer involves a loss of melanocytes from the basal layer which cannot be reversed once it has occurred in the tissue at any point afterwards"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Scarring is a dermal repair process, so a lesion confined above the junction has nothing to scar. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the tissue basis of a papule?","choices":["Separation of epidermis from dermis with fluid accumulation","Focal loss of the epidermis that does not penetrate below the dermal-epidermal junction beneath it","Epidermal hyperplasia with hyperkeratosis, over dermal inflammation and dense collagen","Fluid containing inflammatory cells collecting within or beneath the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A papule is an elevated solid lesion under 5 millimeters, built from epidermal hyperplasia with hyperkeratosis over dermal inflammation, as in seborrheic keratosis. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the primary component of the dermis?","choices":["Collagen, produced by fibroblasts, providing tensile strength","Keratin, produced by keratinocytes, providing an impermeable barrier","Elastin, produced by mast cells, providing tensile strength","Melanin, produced by melanocytes, providing ultraviolet protection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Collagen is the primary component of the dermis; fibroblasts produce it, and it provides the skin's tensile strength. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes epidermal cell turnover?","choices":["Turnover ceases after adolescence","Every 30 to 60 days, and turnover is more rapid in younger patients","Every 24 to 48 hours, with no age dependence","Every 6 to 12 months, and considerably more rapid in older patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epidermal cells turn over every 30 to 60 days, rising from the basement membrane zone to shed, and turnover is faster in younger patients. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How does the mechanism of a hypopigmented macule such as vitiligo differ from that of a hyperpigmented macule?","choices":["Loss of melanocytes, against increased melanin production in the basal layer of the epidermis","Increased melanin production within the epidermis, as against loss of melanocytes entirely","Capillary rupture with blood deposition in the dermis","Dilatation and thinning of superficial dermal vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vitiligo reflects loss of melanocytes, while hyperpigmented macules such as ephelides, café au lait spots and Mongolian spots reflect increased basal melanin. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the tissue distinction between an erosion and an ulcer?","choices":["An erosion stays above the dermal-epidermal junction; an ulcer loses epidermis AND dermis, destroying collagen with inflammatory infiltrate","An erosion involves both the epidermis and the dermis beneath it; an ulcer is limited to the epidermis alone and spares the dermal-epidermal junction entirely, so it never destroys collagen at all","An erosion is fluid-filled and an ulcer is solid","An erosion is a primary lesion and an ulcer is a secondary lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The junction is the boundary that separates them, and crossing it is what brings collagen destruction into play. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
